From d36089459ee566c46c22a9bcc6ed089f1d9a14c0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 07:30:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] Desktop: request deadline follow-ups (upload budget,
 trial-deadline trade-off, late-answer test)

Follow-ups from the review of PR #1955 (total per-attempt request deadline,
20 s down-gate trial deadline).

1. Uploads: the total deadline also bounded request bodies at the JSON
   timeout (30 s, 60 s stretched), so a 25 MB kanban attachment on a ~2 Mbps
   link (~100 s) that used to finish under the idle timer now failed.
   `deadline.arm(req, reject, bodyBytes)` grows the total budget by the time
   the body needs at 1 Mbit/s (requestDeadlineBudgetMs: timeoutMs +
   bytes / 125 per ms; 25 MB -> +210 s). The idle timer stays at the
   caller's timeout, so a stalled upload still fails fast, and its error now
   names the idle timeout instead of the total budget. fetchJson and
   fetchPublicJson pass `body?.length`.
2. remote-down-gate.ts: a comment on REMOTE_DOWN_TRIAL_DEADLINE_MS makes the
   trade-off explicit: 20 s is shorter than a trial request's own budget, so
   a trial still in flight is rejected to its caller while the server may
   still apply it (a non-idempotent mutation included); the abandoned
   request keeps running and a late answer still reopens the gate.
3. Test: the abandoned trial's late success reopens the gate at once
   (fresh sentEpoch), without waiting out the escalated backoff.

Tests: an upload whose transfer outlasts the JSON timeout completes within
its body budget (RED before: timed out at 150 ms); the budget arithmetic for
a 25 MB attachment; the same transfer without a body allowance still times
out; a stalled upload is still cut by the idle timer; main.ts arms with the
body size (RED before).

Card: t_01a1d3cca6f20650 (follow-up to t_01a1d3b1a4a9d420, PR #1955).
